  [FAIL] K02.6 CLAIM UNDER TEST: that the isolated deep-MOND + Lambda turnaround radius, which is what item 80 actually proposed, predicts the Local Group's zero-velocity radius.  It does not -- it over-predicts by a factor 2 to 3 on both footings.  This check is written to FAIL if the item's own prediction is wrong, and it fails   (isolated-MOND predicts canonical: 1.90 Mpc, alt: 1.89 Mpc against 0.88 measured)
  [PASS] K02.7 the EFE-quenched branch -- the regime check K02.5 says is the applicable one -- reproduces the measured R_0 within 0.15 dex on at least one footing, with no dark matter and no fitted parameter   (canonical: pred 1.06 Mpc, -0.078 dex, alt: pred 1.08 Mpc, -0.087 dex)

  THE REFORMULATED STATEMENT, which is what survives:  the dynamical-to-baryonic mass ratio inferred at the
  zero-velocity surface must equal the EXTERNAL-FIELD boost, M_dyn/M_b = nu(g_N,ext/a_0)(1 - L/3), with
  g_ext measured from the system's OWN peculiar velocity.  For the Local Group:
    canonical  M_dyn(from R_0) = 1.352e+12 Msun  ->  M_dyn/M_b =  12.37   predicted nu(e_N)(1-L/3) =  21.26   ratio 0.58 (-0.235 dex)
    alt        M_dyn(from R_0) = 1.524e+12 Msun  ->  M_dyn/M_b =  13.94   predicted nu(e_N)(1-L/3) =  25.47   ratio 0.55 (-0.262 dex)
    LambdaCDM's answer to the same question is the cosmic baryon share, M_dyn/M_b = 1/f_b = 6.4,
    which is the SAME for every system and carries no dependence on the peculiar velocity.  That is the
    discriminator: the framework says the ratio must FALL as v_pec rises, roughly as v_pec^(-1/2).

